{"name":"Piano Keys","hex":"#eee5d4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"piano keys","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The large white striped section at the ends of a runway, used as a landing aiming point and as a distance indicator."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/piano_keys"}],"entries":[{"title":"Key (instrument)","blocks":[{"text":"A key is a component of a musical instrument, the purpose and function of which depends on the instrument. However, the term is most often used in the context of keyboard instruments, in which case it refers to the exterior part of the instrument that the player physically interacts in the process of sound production."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Steinway_Schriftzug.jpg/330px-Steinway_Schriftzug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key_(instrument)","wikidata":"Q1117156"},{"title":"Piano","blocks":[{"text":"A piano is a keyboard instrument that produces sound when its keys are pressed, activating an action mechanism where hammers strike strings. Modern pianos have a row of 88 black and white keys—with the exception of the Bosendörfer and Stuart & Sons pianos—and tuned to a chromatic scale in equal temperament. A musician who specializes in piano is called a pianist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Steinway_Vienna_002.JPG/330px-Steinway_Vienna_002.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piano","wikidata":"Q5994"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Clarinette_auriculaire_droit.jpg/500px-Clarinette_auriculaire_droit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Clarinette_auriculaire_droit.jpg/1280px-Clarinette_auriculaire_droit.jpg?utm_so